                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This wasn't quite what I was expecting when I saw a game show up on Steam based on one of my tutorial series. Sure, I figured it would be quite similar in concept and overall feel, but this is kind of silly. \n\nThe changes from the basic prototype that I created in the series are pretty lacking. You have a few more levels, but the new levels are not anything special. A few lines of the player movement code has been adjusted slightly, but again, that's nothing. This is pretty much my game, you used my color scheme, you used my models, you used my code, and you even used my name. You had all that given to you, but you still couldn't pull out a more satisfying attempt at a rage-inducer?\n\nThere's no feeling of reward when you complete a level, there's no feeling of defeat when you die, and there sure isn't a feeling of \"I can't stop until I've beaten just one more level!\"\n\nIf you have so much given to you from that start, I'd think you would have the time to fix some of the issues as well. And what's up with the timer and coin counter still just being thrown into the top left corner? I did that in the series because it didn't matter as a learning experience, not because it looks good. \n\nYou took what I said too literally. When I said \"take what you learn here and make an actual game,\" I meant make an actual game. Not download my assets and SOMEHOW get it published on Steam. This isn't worth near what you're charging for it. Look at the other games in the same price range. You put a week of work into this game and you're charging a pretty penny for it, it isn't going to work out. \n\nI hate that the game isn't good. I really do. I had a video ready to go showing the game off, getting set for the big key giveaway, but then I realized it isn't worth me doing the video or the giveaway. I'm sorry, but you have a lot of work to do before this game is decent.",
